In article <1992Aug19.173356.19734@ncsu.edu> mrmarcel@eos.ncsu.edu (MICHAEL RONN MARCELAIS) writes:
>Well, its not Amiga graphics, but there is a program that I wrote
>called CHRHACK that alters the default font so that the symbols used
>in the game are 'graphic' based.  (Like realistic dungeon walls, altars
>that look like altars, etc.)  It should be available at places like
>wuarchive, and it was posted to comp.binaries.ibm.pc about three months
>ago.
>

This was a very nice program, BTW.

It took advantage of the fact that certain objects in the game could be 
specified to be represented by different characters with the GRAPHICS command
in the configuration.

I thought that it would be even nicer, though, if everything could be redefined
to be represented by different characters--you, monsters, objects, etc.  Then,
you could have everything changed by a program like this to look different by
using some otherwise unused character to represent them (usually the ones above
chr(127)).

It would require some cooperation from the programmers, either from the dev
team or by some ambitious third party to allow this; you can't simply define
every 'k' to look like a kobold, because then the messages would be unreadable
since they contain such characters too.  You would definitely need an option to
define these things away from characters already being used for other purposes.

I think a separate command in the configuration should be used for this... the
GRAPHICS command could be used for what it is now, and a new command could be
used for this modification.  Otherwise, the GRAPHICS command line might get
painfully long.

I wanted to try this, but all I have is Turbo C 2.0, which doesn't support
overlays, which you need to put all the neat features in.  
